course: everyday
lesson: 281
title: Advert tricks and offers that are too good to be true
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Adverts use clever tricks to make you want things. Food is styled and photographed to look perfect; «Hurry, only today!» rushes you so you stop thinking; «new and improved» may mean only a new box; companies pay films to show their drinks (product placement); and tiny writing like «results may vary» hides the real limits.
explain: A good habit beats all these tricks: slow down, read the label, ask what really changed, and wait a few days before buying. If you still want it after a week, it may be a real wish, not just an advert's push.
explain: Some offers are not adverts but traps. You cannot win a contest you never entered, nobody gives away free game coins for your password, and «get rich quick» offers are almost always tricks.
explain: Example: a boy in Ontario gets a pop-up: «Congratulations! You are our 1,000,000th visitor! Click to claim your iPhone!». The same pop-up is shown to everyone. He closes the page and tells his dad, and nothing is lost.
explain: The rule of thumb: if it sounds too good to be true, it almost always is. Close it and tell an adult.
fact: A loot box is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you will get, and games make it exciting so you spend more.
fact: Food in adverts is styled and photographed to look perfect; real food is usually smaller and messier.
fact: For claims like «9 out of 10 dentists recommend», ask how many were asked and who paid for the survey; asking only a few people proves very little.
fact: «Hurry, only today!» is a trick to rush people, because rushing stops them thinking clearly.
fact: «New and improved» may only mean a new box, so ask what has really changed.
fact: Product placement is when companies pay to show their products in films and shows; it is advertising hidden in entertainment.
fact: Words like «with real fruit» can make sugary food sound healthy, so check the sugar on the label with an adult.
fact: Small print like «results may vary» or «not typical» means most people will not get the results shown.
fact: If you want something just because everyone has it, wait a few days; the feeling often fades.
fact: You cannot win a contest you never entered; «you have won» messages are tricks to steal information or money.
fact: Never use «generators» or give your password for free Robux, V-Bucks or coins; they steal accounts or install harmful software.
fact: Only trade game items through official in-game trading; outside trades are often scams where you give and get nothing.
fact: Real money takes real work; «get rich quick» offers are almost always tricks, so ignore them and tell an adult.
fact: Very cheap famous brands on a strange website may be fake shops that steal card details, so ask a parent to check.
exercise: A game offers a «mystery chest» for real money that might hold a rare prize. What is it like? | Gambling: you pay without knowing what you get | A fair shop where you choose the item you want and see it first | A free gift from the game makers | Saving money in a bank
exercise: The burger in the advert is tall and shiny, but the real one is flat. Why? | Advert food is styled and photographed to look perfect | The restaurant ran out of good burgers that day, so you got a flat one | Burgers shrink on the way to your table | You were given a children's size by mistake
exercise: An advert says «9 out of 10 dentists recommend Sparkle Paste». What is a smart question? | How many dentists were asked, and who paid for it? | What colour is the toothpaste? | Which dentist is the tenth? | Is it sold in big tubes?
exercise: A website shows a countdown: «Only 5 minutes left at this price!». What is wise? | Take your time, because rushing stops clear thinking | Buy at once before it ends | Ask a friend to buy two | Refresh the page again and again to make the timer longer
exercise: A crisp packet says «NEW and IMPROVED!». What should you ask? | What has actually changed? | How much bigger is the bag now? | Is it now a healthy food for kids? | Was the old one bad for you?
exercise: In a film, the hero drinks a famous brand of soda, with the label facing the camera. What is this? | Product placement, paid advertising inside the film | Just a lucky accident | Proof the actor loves the soda | A rule that every film has to show a drink in at least one scene
exercise: A cereal box says «made with real fruit». What should you check with an adult? | The sugar on the nutrition label | The cartoon on the front of the box | How many pieces of fruit are pictured | Whether the box can be recycled at home
exercise: A weight-loss advert shows amazing results with «*results not typical» in tiny writing. What does it mean? | Most people will not get the results shown | Everyone will get even better results than the ones in the advert | The product works only at night | The results are guaranteed by doctors
exercise: Everyone in your class has a fidget toy and you suddenly want one too. What is a wise step? | Wait a few days and see if you still want it | Buy it today before every shop sells out | Ask for three to be the best | Borrow money from a friend now
exercise: A message says you have won a free iPhone and must enter your details. What do you do? | Do not click; close it and tell an adult | Enter your details quickly before it ends | Forward it to friends so they can win too | Reply and ask which colour of phone you get
exercise: A website promises free Robux if you type in your username and password. What is it? | A scam that steals accounts or installs harmful software | A secret gift from the game company to its most loyal players | A fair reward for loyal players | A competition for the best player
exercise: Someone online says «Give me your rare skin first, then I'll give you two». What is right? | Only use official in-game trading, never trade outside it | Give it, because two skins beat one | Give it if they seem friendly | Ask for three before you give it
exercise: A stranger online says you can earn £500 a day by sending them your bank details. What should you do? | Ignore it and tell an adult; it is a trick | Send the details to start earning | Ask your friends to join first | Try it once with a small amount, just to see if it works
exercise: A website you've never heard of sells famous trainers for $10. What is likely? | It may be a fake shop that steals card details | It is a lucky bargain to buy fast | The trainers are slightly used | The brand is closing down
exercise: A pop-up says «Congratulations! You are the 1,000,000th visitor!». What is true? | It is a classic scam shown to everyone; close it | You are really the millionth visitor and have won a big prize | You must click to get your prize | It is safe because it congratulates you
test: Why do games make opening loot boxes so flashy and exciting? | So that players keep spending more money | So that players can learn the names of all the bright colours | Because the prizes are always rare | Because the game is free
test: A pizza on TV has cheese stretching a metre long, but yours does not. Was the advert showing a normal pizza? | No, advert food is styled to look perfect | Yes, your pizza is just broken or was cooked badly | Yes, but only at the restaurant | No, the TV pizza was smaller
test: A shampoo says «4 out of 5 people prefer it» but only five people were asked. Which is TRUE? | Five people is too small a group to prove much | Four out of five is always a strong fact, however many people asked | Small surveys are the most accurate | All surveys ask millions of people
test: A girl in Wellington rushes to buy a game skin because of a «last chance» timer. What trick was used? | Making her rush so she would not think clearly | Giving her a real once-in-a-lifetime offer that will never return | Helping her save money | Giving her a free trial
test: A box looks different but the toy inside is exactly the same. The box says «NEW!». What happened? | Only the packaging changed to make you buy again | The toy is secretly better inside | New means it is free | The old toy was recalled for safety, so this one is new
test: You get a text: «You've won a $1000 gift card! Claim now!» but you never entered any contest. What does that tell you? | You cannot win a contest you never entered, so it is a trick | You must have been entered by a friend who wanted to surprise you | Gift cards are given out randomly to everyone | You should claim it fast before it expires
test: A YouTube video says a «coin generator» works if you log in with your game account. What is most likely to happen? | Your account gets stolen | You get endless free coins | The game gives you a badge | Nothing at all happens to it
test: A man at the park says he knows a way for kids to get rich in a week. What is right? | Walk away and tell an adult; real money takes real work | Listen carefully, give him your address and bring some money | Bring your piggy bank tomorrow | Ask him to show you somewhere private
test: You still really want a skateboard a whole month after seeing an advert. What does that suggest? | It is probably a real wish, not just an advert's push | The advert tricked you even more and you should forget it now | You should never buy anything | You must buy two of them
test: game says 50 coins for mystery box, worth it? | It's like gambling, you pay not knowing what you get | Yes, mystery boxes always hold the best items, they're worth it | Yes, they're cheaper than buying items | Only if you buy ten at once
test: msg says i won a ipad but i never entered anything | It's a trick, you can't win a contest you never entered | Lucky you, reply with your address | Click the link quick before it ends | Ask them to send it to school instead of to your home address
test: site gives free robux if i put my password in, legit? | No, it's a scam to steal your account | Yes, if the site has nice pictures and lots of happy reviews | Yes, but only once a week | Only if a friend used it first
test: burger in the ad is massive, mine's tiny n squashed. why | Ad food is styled and shot to look perfect | They gave you the wrong burger by mistake today | The ad shows the real size of every single burger | Burgers shrink a lot in the minutes after they are made
explain: A boy in Canada spent all his birthday money on loot boxes in one evening hoping for a rare skin; he never got it and the money was gone.
explain: A girl in England's mum clicked a «free trial» for a beauty product; it kept taking money from her card every month until she noticed months later.
fact: Loot boxes work like gambling, and some children have spent hundreds without their parents knowing.
fact: Many «free trials» turn into payments that repeat every month unless someone cancels in time.
test: free trial just needs mums card number, ok? | Ask a parent first, free trials often start charging | Yes, it's free so it's fine | Yes, if you cancel some day | Yes, just copy the number, she'll never notice a free trial
test: 'only today!!' sale on these trainers should i beg dad now | No, rushing stops you thinking, the offer is a trick to hurry you | Yes, the price will rise forever after today and you'll miss it | Yes, sales never come back | Yes, tomorrow they'll be gone
test: spent all my money on loot boxes, got nothing good | Tell a parent; loot boxes are like gambling | Spend more, the next one is bound to be good, they always pay out | Borrow money to try again | Hide it so no one knows
test: did u hear a kid "lost his whole account with 3 years of skins" cos he typed his password into a free robux site. lesson? | Never give your password to any free coins generator | Only use generators that look official | Change your password straight after using one, then it's safe | Free robux sites are fine on weekends
test: my cousin says a girl "won an iPhone" from a random text and her mum "lost thousands". what's the bit to doubt | That she won anything; you can't win a contest you never entered | That texts can be scams, that's the bit that sounds made up | That scams can take money | That phones get messages
test: kids say the boy who got scammed in a trade is "so stupid lol". pass it on? | No, don't mock him; just trade only through official in-game trading | Yes, he should have known better | Yes, and tell everyone his username so they can laugh at him too | Yes, as a warning to everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a loot box? | A purchase of unknown contents, comparable to gambling | A guaranteed purchase of a chosen item | A free reward granted by the makers for good conduct in the game | A storage container for game items
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the phrase «Hurry, only today!» in advertising. | To rush persons so that they do not reflect clearly | To inform persons of the closing time of the shop | To guarantee that the price will rise tomorrow | To reward persons who arrive early
exam: In respect of the small print «results may vary» or «not typical», select the response consistent with the established facts. | Most persons will not obtain the results shown | All persons are guaranteed to obtain the results shown | The results shown are typical of every customer | The product has since been withdrawn from sale entirely
exam: It is given that a message announces that the recipient has won a contest never entered. State the correct interpretation. | It is a trick to obtain information or money | It is a genuine prize awarded at random | It is a reward for loyal customers | It is an official notice requiring a prompt reply
